From 7aaa7052d71a3074f8713b36866ad27e3d8be5b6 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Justo Tapiador <justo.tapiador@yahoo.com>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 23:20:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] feat(auth): the sign-in round trip - one link, back where you
 were (F19 follow-up)

F19 shared the cookie; the flow still broke on the public site. The
sign-in link on public/index.jhs pointed at the CMS host's /login
with no way back, the forms' redirect field only accepted
same-origin paths, and the browser's CSP swallowed the cross-host
303 whole. Three pieces close the trip:

- login_url, the new template global: cms_origin +
  /login?redirect=<this page's absolute URL> while the CMS has its
  own host (the relative /login?redirect=<path> on the single-host
  server, the shape every phase before the split served).
  public/index.jhs writes <?= login_url ?> and nothing else.
- The returning redirect: the login, register and logout forms now
  also accept absolute http/https URLs whose host is one the server
  itself serves - the request's own host, every host the live vhost
  table maps, and everything under [auth] cookie_domain: exactly the
  family the shared session covers, because a redirect to a host
  the cookie cannot follow to would land the visitor signed-out
  where they started. Foreign domains, javascript: and every other
  scheme still fall back to / (the open-redirect guard of every
  phase before), and the failure bounce composes with the absolute
  target's query string.
- form-action, the browser's half: a form's 303 may only cross the
  Host line when the CSP lets the redirect target through, and the
  shipped form-action 'self' pinned it to one origin - the sign-in
  POST succeeded, the cookie was stored, and the navigation never
  happened (not one follow-up request; Chrome only notes it in its
  console). While the family is non-empty, the configured policy's
  form-action 'self' is widened automatically with the same origins
  the redirect allowlist trusts (cookie_domain and subdomains, every
  mapped host, the [tls] scheme and [server] port); a form-action
  naming other sources, or none, ships as written.

Also: the cookie_domain validation message no longer suggests the
one-label "localhost" it rejects.

tests/shared_session.rs grows the round trip: the sign-in link
carrying the return page, the 303 back to the main host (failure
bounce composed), the off-family fallback (the one-label localhost
split pinned as the local-dev trap it is) and the widened policy,
with the single-host control. The live-browser walk against a real
Chrome: sign in from app.localhost's link, land back on the very
page signed-in, the shared cookie stored under the domain, sign
out, anonymous again - 15/15.
